  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nLate Summer Heat Wave\nUpper-level ridging remains dominant across the Midwest, \nanchored by a robust 596 dam 500mb high centered over the mid- \nMississippi and Tennessee Valleys. This ridge axis is projected\nto shift toward the southeastern CONUS by late week. Guidance \ncontinues to advertise 850mb thermal ridge temperatures of \n23-26C over the region through Saturday, well above the 90th \npercentile, following this morning's 12Z sounding of 22C. \n\nSurface temperatures will run well above normal through the \nweekend. Dew points are expected to persist in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s, though deep mixing into a drier column and a seasonal\ndrop in evapotranspiration will help keep them somewhat in \ncheck. Even so, temperatures have a solid chance of reaching \ntriple digits. NBM probabilities for hitting 100 degrees are \nhighest (40-80%) today along and south of a Springfield to Casey\nline, lowering slightly to 30-60% across the southwestern CWA \nThursday and Friday. Peak heat indices will generally range near\n105 degrees through Friday and around 100 degrees Saturday. Due\nto the prolonged period of oppressive conditions, the Heat \nAdvisory has been upgraded to an Extreme Heat Warning through \nFriday.\n\nConvective Risk & Storm Potential\nPrecipitation chances will stay mostly confined to northern \nIllinois and the Great Lakes near the jet stream through the\nremainder of the week. With surface temperatures approaching \nconvective thresholds daily, an isolated pop-up afternoon or \nevening thunderstorm cannot be completely ruled out, though \noverall probability remains under 20% due to nebulous and weak \nsynoptic forcing. Models like the RRFS continue to highlight \nthis pop-up potential. Any storms that manage to develop on \nThursday would be supported by MLCAPE of 2000-2500 J/kg and \ndeep-layer shear of 25-30 kt. MLCAPE increases to 2500-3000 J/kg\non Friday, but noticeably weaker shear points toward a more \npulse-severe mode. \n\nWeekend Pattern Shift\nLate this week into the weekend, a closed upper low near Hudson\nBay will dig toward New England, deepening a longwave trough \nover the eastern U.S. This pushes the upper ridge axis west over\nthe central Plains and places central Illinois in a northwest \nflow regime. Meanwhile, Canadian high pressure over Quebec will \npush a backdoor cold front south into Illinois, providing a \nfocus for weekend rain chances (20-30%). Temperatures will \ngradually cool from the 90s ahead of the boundary down into the \nlower-to-mid 80s behind it. Because this transition unfolds \nslowly, a thermal gradient across central Illinois will persist \ninto the first half of next week.",
